WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.230
IEC 62443 organizes technical security objectives into seven foundational requirements.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.820
Together they provide a balanced view of identity, authorization, integrity,

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.650
confidentiality, communication, detection, response, and availability.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.135
The required strength of each objective depends on the zone, conduit, risk,

00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:19.605
and selected security level.

00:00:19.605 --> 00:00:23.485
Identification and authentication control establishes who or what is requesting access and

00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:24.880
verifies that identity.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.170
Use control determines what an authenticated user, device, or process is allowed to do.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.460
Industrial systems may have technical limitations,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.050
so architecture and managed gateways may need to enforce controls that individual devices

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:36.640
cannot.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.555
System integrity protects systems, software, configurations,

00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:42.640
and communications from unauthorized or accidental change.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.290
Data confidentiality protects information from unauthorized disclosure where that

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.535
disclosure creates risk.

00:00:48.535 --> 00:00:49.845
Confidentiality matters in OT,

00:00:49.845 --> 00:00:54.560
but it must be implemented without undermining required visibility, timing, or recovery.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.160
Restricted data flow limits communication between zones and through conduits.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.920
It supports segmentation and containment by allowing necessary flows while denying

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.560
unnecessary paths.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.270
Timely response to events addresses the ability to detect, record, communicate,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.860
and respond to security-relevant activity.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.915
Useful monitoring requires ownership, context, and practiced procedures.

00:01:13.915 --> 00:01:17.085
Resource availability protects the capacity and functions needed for safe,

00:01:17.085 --> 00:01:18.080
reliable operation.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.250
This includes resilience against resource exhaustion, component failure,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.920
and disruptive security controls.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.880
Redundancy, backup, recovery, and graceful degradation may all contribute.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.640
The foundational requirements should be engineered as a system.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.700
Strong authentication without availability planning can lock operators out during an

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.645
emergency.

00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:37.270
Excellent logging without response ownership produces little value.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.770
The objective is a set of mutually supporting controls aligned with the industrial

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.330
process.
